### What changed & why it matters

| Change | Security / Stability Benefit |
|--------|------------------------------|
| **Helmet** – sets a collection of HTTP security headers (CSP, HSTS, X‑Frame‑Options, etc.). | Mitigates click‑jacking, XSS, MIME‑sniffing, and forces HTTPS (if you configure HSTS). |
| **CORS** with configurable whitelist (`CORS_ORIGIN`) | Prevents browsers from sending authenticated requests from unauthorized origins. |
| **express‑rate‑limit** | Thwarts brute‑force credential attacks and basic DoS attempts. |
| **express.json({ limit })** | Stops oversized payload attacks that could exhaust memory. |
| **xss‑clean** | Sanitises user‑supplied strings preventing reflected XSS if data ever ends up in HTML. |
| **Morgan logging** | Gives you an audit trail; use a proper logger (Winston/Pino) in production. |
| **Joi validation** (example) | Guarantees that only well‑formed input reaches your business logic, protecting against injection attacks. |
| **Central error middleware** (no stack traces in prod) | Prevents leaking internal implementation details while still giving useful error messages to developers. |
| **Graceful shutdown** | Allows in‑flight requests to finish, improves reliability during deployments. |
| **Environment‑driven configuration** (port, CORS origins, rate‑limit) | Makes the app portable across dev / staging / prod without code changes. |
| **Export‑only pattern** | Keeps unit‑tests fast and isolated; the HTTP server starts only when the file is the entry point. |

