### Why this approach is safe and aligns with security best‑practices

| Practice | How it’s addressed in the script |
|----------|---------------------------------|
| **Never log secrets** | The script deliberately never prints the value of `process.env.*`. Only success/failure messages are shown. |
| **Fail‑closed** | If an env var is missing, a warning is issued and the specific check is skipped – you won’t get a false “OK” because the code used a fallback value. |
| **Least privilege testing** | Each test issues the smallest possible request (`SELECT 1`, `ping`, `/ping` endpoint) that does not modify data. |
| **Graceful error handling** | All async operations are wrapped in `try/catch` blocks; the process never crashes because of an unhandled rejection. |
| **Config‑driven** | The script reads the exact same env variables the app uses, so you can test any environment (`.env`, `.env.test`, CI environment) without hard‑coding values. |
| **Modular** | You can add or remove sections for other services (e.g., AWS, SendGrid) without touching the rest of the script. |
| **No side‑effects** | No writes to the database, no POST/PUT requests – purely read‑only validation. |

